# The pandemic as a case study in authority claiming expertise

Avik Jain, with Claude Opus 5.5, 2026-11-19.

Avik's thesis (21:37): the WHO "killed with bad unscientifically grounded recommendations"; the pandemic is "a tragic case study in authority claiming expertise and bullshitting." "Almost none of the statements attributed to experts at the time were scientifically grounded or had an epistemic basis beyond consensus opinions on what to convince the public of."

The record below argues that thesis case by case. Each case is the operation *Alignment Is Standpoint* names (§3.3, §4.3): an authority replaces the record with a minted verdict, and enforcement takes the place of evidence.

## 1. The authority relayed the censor

On 14 January 2020 the WHO tweeted: "Preliminary investigations conducted by the Chinese authorities have found no clear evidence of human-to-human transmission of the novel #coronavirus (2019-nCoV) identified in #Wuhan, #China." The source was a government that was censoring Wuhan doctors who had known of human-to-human transmission since late December, Dr. Li Wenliang among them. The same day, the WHO's own expert, Dr. Maria Van Kerkhove, warned that "we need to prepare ourselves" for mass human transmission; the Guardian (Julian Borger) reported that internal discomfort with such warnings prompted the "no clear evidence" tweet for "balance". The public statement was not the institution's own scientific judgment. It was the censoring state's claim, relayed.

## 2. A "FACT" that was false, held for nearly two years

On 28 March 2020 the WHO tweeted: "FACT: #COVID19 is NOT airborne", on the ground that droplets "are too heavy to hang in the air." An NIH study had already found the virus could persist in air. In July 2020, 238 scientists wrote to the WHO asking it to recognize airborne transmission. The WHO first applied the word "airborne" to COVID-19 on its transmission page in December 2021. Aerosol scientists now hold aerosols to be almost certainly the main route. For nearly two years, official advice directed the public at surfaces and hand-washing instead of ventilation.

## 3. Persuasion presented as science

US Surgeon General Jerome Adams, 29 February 2020: "Seriously people- STOP BUYING MASKS! They are NOT effective in preventing general public from catching #Coronavirus, but if healthcare providers can't get them to care for sick patients, it puts them and our communities at risk!" The efficacy claim and its real motive, a supply shortage, appear in one tweet. The WHO and CDC likewise did not recommend masks for the healthy public at first. The CDC reversed in April 2020 and the WHO in June 2020.

## 4. A rule that "sort of just appeared"

Asked in January 2024 for the basis of the six-foot distancing rule that governed schools and businesses, Anthony Fauci testified: "I don't recall. It sort of just appeared." In public testimony on 3 June 2024 he clarified that there "wasn't a controlled trial." UCLA economist Andrew Atkeson: "We never did the study about what works." Former FDA commissioner Scott Gottlieb called it "probably the single most costly intervention the CDC recommended that was consistently applied throughout the pandemic."

## 5. Censorship of the question itself

In February 2021 Facebook added claims that the virus was man-made or came from a lab to its list of removable "debunked" claims. It lifted the ban on 26–27 May 2021, the day after President Biden ordered intelligence agencies to investigate origins including a laboratory accident. A hypothesis no one had disproved was removed from public discussion, and it returned by presidential directive, not by evidence.

## The pattern

In each case the words that were repeated were wrong, and the words that were true were silenced, delayed or labeled misinformation: the Wuhan doctors, Van Kerkhove's warning, the aerosol scientists, open inquiry into origins. Harm followed from the removal of words, never from their presence. By the standard of knowledge as the absence of veils, the pandemic's official epistemology was largely veil, and consensus about what to tell the public stood in for evidence about what was true.

